164 | [0.1s]           It works!
165 | [0.1s]         </div>
166 | [0.1s]         <div class="content_section_text">
167 | [0.1s]           <p>
168 | [0.1s]                 This is the default welcome page used to test the correct 
169 | [0.1s]                 operation of the Apache2 server after installation on Debian systems.
170 | [0.1s]                 If you can read this page, it means that the Apache HTTP server installed at
171 | [0.1s]                 this site is working properly. You should <b>replace this file</b> (located at
172 | [0.1s]                 <tt>/var/www/html/index.html</tt>) before continuing to operate your HTTP server.
173 | [0.1s]           </p>
174 | [0.1s]           <p>
175 | [0.1s]                 If you are a normal user of this web site and don't know what this page is
176 | [0.1s]                 about, this probably means that the site is currently unavailable due to
177 | [0.1s]                 maintenance.
178 | [0.1s]                 If the problem persists, please contact the site's administrator.
179 | [0.1s]           </p>
180 | [0.1s]         </div>
181 | [0.1s]         <div class="section_header">
182 | [0.1s]           <div id="changes"></div>
183 | [0.1s]                 Configuration Overview
184 | [0.1s]         </div>
185 | [0.1s]         <div class="content_section_text">
186 | [0.1s]           <p>
187 | [0.1s]                 Debian's Apache2 default configuration is different from the
188 | [0.1s]                 upstream default configuration, and split into several files optimized for
189 | [0.1s]                 interaction with Debian tools. The configuration system is
190 | [0.1s]                 <b>fully documented in
191 | [0.1s]                 /usr/share/doc/apache2/README.Debian.gz</b>. Refer to this for the full
192 | [0.1s]                 documentation. Documentation for the web server itself can be
193 | [0.1s]                 found by accessing the <a href="/manual">manual</a> if the <tt>apache2-doc</tt>
194 | [0.1s]                 package was installed on this server.
195 | [0.1s]           </p>
196 | [0.1s]           <p>
197 | [0.1s]                 The configuration layout for an Apache2 web server installation on Debian systems is as follows:
198 | [0.1s]           </p>
199 | [0.1s]           <pre>
200 | [0.1s] /etc/apache2/
201 | [0.1s] |-- apache2.conf
202 | [0.1s] |       `--  ports.conf
203 | [0.1s] |-- mods-enabled
204 | [0.1s] |       |-- *.load
205 | [0.1s] |       `-- *.conf
206 | [0.1s] |-- conf-enabled
207 | [0.1s] |       `-- *.conf
208 | [0.1s] |-- sites-enabled
209 | [0.1s] |       `-- *.conf
210 | [0.1s]           </pre>
211 | [0.1s]           <ul>
212 | [0.1s]                         <li>
213 | [0.1s]                            <tt>apache2.conf</tt> is the main configuration
214 | [0.1s]                            file. It puts the pieces together by including all remaining configuration
215 | [0.1s]                            files when starting up the web server.
216 | [0.1s]                         </li>
217 | [0.1s]                         <li>
218 | [0.1s]                            <tt>ports.conf</tt> is always included from the
219 | [0.1s]                            main configuration file. It is used to determine the listening ports for
220 | [0.1s]                            incoming connections, and this file can be customized anytime.
221 | [0.1s]                         </li>
222 | [0.1s]                         <li>
223 | [0.1s]                            Configuration files in the <tt>mods-enabled/</tt>,
224 | [0.1s]                            <tt>conf-enabled/</tt> and <tt>sites-enabled/</tt> directories contain
225 | [0.1s]                            particular configuration snippets which manage modules, global configuration
226 | [0.1s]                            fragments, or virtual host configurations, respectively.
227 | [0.1s]                         </li>
228 | [0.1s]                         <li>
229 | [0.1s]                            They are activated by symlinking available
230 | [0.1s]                            configuration files from their respective
231 | [0.1s]                            *-available/ counterparts. These should be managed
232 | [0.1s]                            by using our helpers
233 | [0.1s]                            <tt>
234 | [0.1s]                                 a2enmod,
235 | [0.1s]                                 a2dismod,
236 | [0.1s]                            </tt>
237 | [0.1s]                            <tt>
238 | [0.1s]                                     a2ensite,
239 | [0.1s]                                 a2dissite,
240 | [0.1s]                             </tt>
241 | [0.1s]                                 and
242 | [0.1s]                            <tt>
243 | [0.1s]                                 a2enconf,
244 | [0.1s]                                 a2disconf
245 | [0.1s]                            </tt>. See their respective man pages for detailed information.
246 | [0.1s]                         </li>
247 | [0.1s]                         <li>
248 | [0.1s]                            The binary is called apache2. Due to the use of
249 | [0.1s]                            environment variables, in the default configuration, apache2 needs to be
250 | [0.1s]                            started/stopped with <tt>/etc/init.d/apache2</tt> or <tt>apache2ctl</tt>.
251 | [0.1s]                            <b>Calling <tt>/usr/bin/apache2</tt> directly will not work</b> with the
252 | [0.1s]                            default configuration.
253 | [0.1s]                         </li>
254 | [0.1s]           </ul>
255 | [0.1s]         </div>
256 | [0.1s]         <div class="section_header">
257 | [0.1s]             <div id="docroot"></div>
258 | [0.1s]                 Document Roots
259 | [0.1s]         </div>
260 | [0.1s]         <div class="content_section_text">
261 | [0.1s]             <p>
262 | [0.1s]                 By default, Debian does not allow access through the web browser to
263 | [0.1s]                 <em>any</em> file apart of those located in <tt>/var/www</tt>,
264 | [0.1s]                 <a href="https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/mod/mod_userdir.html" rel="nofollow">public_html</a>
265 | [0.1s]                 directories (when enabled) and <tt>/usr/share</tt> (for web
266 | [0.1s]                 applications). If your site is using a web document root
267 | [0.1s]                 located elsewhere (such as in <tt>/srv</tt>) you may need to whitelist your
268 | [0.1s]                 document root directory in <tt>/etc/apache2/apache2.conf</tt>.
269 | [0.1s]             </p>
270 | [0.1s]             <p>
271 | [0.1s]                 The default Debian document root is <tt>/var/www/html</tt>. You
272 | [0.1s]                 can make your own virtual hosts under /var/www. This is different
273 | [0.1s]                 to previous releases which provides better security out of the box.
274 | [0.1s]             </p>
275 | [0.1s]         </div>
276 | [0.1s]         <div class="section_header">
277 | [0.1s]           <div id="bugs"></div>
278 | [0.1s]                 Reporting Problems
279 | [0.1s]         </div>
280 | [0.1s]         <div class="content_section_text">
281 | [0.1s]           <p>
282 | [0.1s]                 Please use the <tt>reportbug</tt> tool to report bugs in the
283 | [0.1s]                 Apache2 package with Debian. However, check <a
284 | [0.1s]                 href="https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/pkgreport.cgi?ordering=normal;archive=0;src=apache2;repeatmerged=0"
285 | [0.1s]                 rel="nofollow">existing bug reports</a> before reporting a new bug.
286 | [0.1s]           </p>
287 | [0.1s]           <p>
288 | [0.1s]                 Please report bugs specific to modules (such as PHP and others)
289 | [0.1s]                 to respective packages, not to the web server itself.
290 | [0.1s]           </p>
